## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

This is the article that closes a loop the whole sub-arc has been circling. Part 4.0 handed the reader the **families** of protective drugs ("super ancillaries"). Part 4.1 told them **when** to reach for one (a marker drifts the wrong way) — and every ladder in 4.1 ended at a physician gate labelled *"which family member = Part 4.2."* This is that article. Its own first sentence names the job: **"the class does the job; the member decides the fit."**

The single idea a reader must *feel* instantly is the one the article italicises and returns to in every section:

> **==Within a class, every member does the same core job. The differences are side-effect profile, half-life, tissue selectivity, drug interactions, and what *else* the molecule happens to do.==** A statin lowers LDL whether it's rosuvastatin or simvastatin — the question is which one wrecks your training with muscle aches. An ARB lowers BP whether it's telmisartan or irbesartan — the question is which *secondary* benefits you actually need, and which are already covered. **Choosing well is a fitting problem, not a ranking problem.**

That is the whole poster's grammar: **same socket, different skirt.** Every drug class is a socket. Its members are interchangeable plugs that all *fit the socket* (all do the job). They differ only in the **spec skirt** below the flange — and choosing means reading those skirts against *your* stack. A generic chat will draw a **pharmacy shelf of pill bottles**, or simply re-typeset the article's comparison tables as an image. Both miss the payload. The payload is **geometry**: identical mounting flanges (the shared job) over divergent spec-fingerprints (the difference), with one part seated and lit and the rest dimmed-but-present, each rejection *explained*.

Four structural truths sit under that thesis, and the instrument has to carry all four:

1. **The stack is one ecosystem, read left-to-right.** The article's master table is an explicit chain: a compound's **family** (3.1) predicts what it does → its **pathway/mechanism** (3.0) determines what it *loads* → the load shows up as a **biomarker** (2.0 / 4.1) → which names the **ancillary** that defends it (4.0). *"Left to right is the whole logic of enhanced harm reduction."* So the bench must **read left→right**: each tray-row enters on the left with the incoming **LOAD** (which compound family strains which system), passes the **BIOMARKER WATCH** socket in the middle, and you **SEAT the matched member** on the right. The poster's reading direction *is* the series' through-line.

2. **You defend by SYSTEM, not by drug — so one well-chosen part covers several compounds.** The article's hardest practical rule: *"You don't run one ancillary per compound — you protect each system against the combined load, which is why the same ARB or statin covers several compounds at once."* In a stack the risks **multiply** (Test, EQ *and* GH all push BP and water at once). The geometric expression is a **CROSS-TIE BUS**: a seated part **fans out** to every load it defends (one statin → lipids + endothelium; tadalafil → BP + prostate + pump + mild diuresis; omega-3 → lipids + neuro). This is the deliberate, recontextualised callback to 4.0's super-ancillary manifold — but here the cross-ties are the mechanism that keeps the **parts-count low**, which is the article's closing anti-over-stacking thesis made structural.

3. **"What's already handled elsewhere" demotes a part from lifeline to nice-to-have.** A uniquely 4.2 move, repeated for telmisartan: its PPAR-γ metabolic edge is *"redundant"* if a GLP-1 is in the stack; its tissue-penetration advantage *"drops from lifeline to nice-to-have"* once rosuvastatin + aspirin + tadalafil are already running, so *"irbesartan + tadalafil is already a rock-solid pairing."* The instrument needs a **REDUNDANCY DIMMER**: when an upstream part is seated, it sends an "already covered" signal that **greys out** a candidate's bonus-spec bar — you watch a member demote itself in real time. This is what "match to what's already handled elsewhere" looks like.

4. **The wrong member doesn't just underperform — sometimes it *sabotages*.** The sharpest case in the article: in the aldosterone-blocker (MRA) tray, **spironolactone** sits right next to the correct pick but *"blocks androgen receptors, causing gyno and anti-androgen effects — it actively sabotages your cycle. Avoid."* The right member (eplerenone) is *selective*; the wrong one fights you. That single part — same tray as the answer, but barbed — is the perfect, on-thesis **single red object** for a *selection* poster: choosing wrong inside a class isn't neutral.

**The single key point to make a reader feel:** *Every drug in a class does the same job — so stop asking "which is strongest" and start asking "which one fits." You read the spec-skirt below the flange (side-effects, half-life, selectivity, interactions, bonus job), you check what's already handled elsewhere in your stack, and you seat ONE part per system — chosen so it covers several compounds at once. The statin split (water-soluble = gentler on muscle) is the cleanest example; spironolactone in the wrong tray is the warning that picking wrong can sabotage you.*

