## 1. The Big Idea

_Choosing an ancillary is not a contest to find the strongest drug — it's a **fitting job**. Every member of a class drops into the **same socket** and does the **same core job**; they differ only in the **spec-skirt** below the flange — side-effects, half-life, tissue selectivity, interactions, and what each one *also* does. So you don't shop for "best." You **seat the one that fits your stack**: the statin that won't ache your muscles, the ARB whose bonus you actually need (and isn't already covered by a GLP-1), the **one** part per system chosen to cover several compounds at once. The poster is a tall **fitment rack** of pull-out member-trays — same flange, different skirt, exactly one part seated and lit per tray with the reason on a chip, the rejects dimmed-but-present with a one-line disqualifier. The statin tray is biggest (the single most useful split there is). And the one red object on the whole page is **spironolactone seated in the wrong tray** — the member that doesn't just underperform but **sabotages your cycle** — because the deepest lesson of the article is that, inside a class, picking wrong is not neutral._

## 2. Visual Metaphor & Structure

A **specification selector / interchangeable-part fitment rack** — the visual language of an engineer's parts cabinet or a component spec-binder, where a family of interchangeable parts shares one mounting interface and you select by reading their differing spec plates. It earns its place five ways:

1. **"Same job, different fit" is a flange-and-skirt, not a ranking.** The article's spine — *every member does the same core job; only the side-spec differs* — becomes literal geometry: every candidate in a tray is drawn with the **identical MOUNTING FLANGE** at the top (so the eye sees "these are interchangeable / they all do the job") and a **divergent SPEC-FINGERPRINT skirt** below it (so the eye sees "this is the only thing you're choosing between"). A reader learns the grammar once and reads all twelve trays the same way.

2. **Seated-and-lit vs dimmed-but-present draws the decision *and its reason*.** In each tray, **one** part is **seated into the socket and illuminated** (the pick) with a reason chip; the others sit **dimmed in the tray**, each wearing a one-line **disqualifier tag** ("simva 80 — diffuses into muscle"; "losartan — weakest, *unless* you're also fighting uric acid"). Rejections stay *visible and explained* — the article never just says "use rosuvastatin," it says *why* the alternatives lose — so the poster must show the bench, not a winner's podium.

3. **Left-load → right-seat draws the whole ecosystem.** Each tray-row reads **left → right**: incoming **LOAD** (compound family · what it loads) → **BIOMARKER WATCH** socket (what you measure) → **SEATED MEMBER** (the matched defense). That is the article's master table — *family → pathway → biomarker → ancillary* — turned into the bench's reading direction.

4. **Cross-tie buses draw "defend by system, not by drug."** A seated part **fans out** along a thin bus to every system/compound it defends. The reader sees one statin feeding lipids *and* endothelium, one tadalafil feeding BP *and* prostate *and* pump — so *"the same ancillary covers several compounds; you protect the system against the combined load"* is structural, and the low parts-count is visibly the *result* of choosing multi-tasking members. (Deliberate callback to 4.0's manifold, recontextualised: there it was a defense note; here it is the selection payoff.)

5. **The redundancy dimmer draws "what's already handled elsewhere."** When an upstream part is seated, it sends an `ALREADY COVERED` pulse down a faint tie that **greys a candidate's bonus-bar** — telmisartan's PPAR-γ bar going dark under a seated GLP-1, its tissue-penetration bar going dark under seated rosuva + aspirin + tadalafil, with the demotion stamped `LIFELINE → NICE-TO-HAVE · irbesartan + tadalafil already holds it.` You *watch* a member talk itself out of the stack.

**The signature teaching device** is the **STATIN TRAY, drawn biggest** and docked as the legend, because the article calls the hydrophilic/lipophilic split *"the single most useful split in the entire ancillary world."* Two members seated side by side over the same lipid socket:

- **ROSUVASTATIN** — a **water-drop** glyph, `HYDROPHILIC`, the **muscle-ache** bar drawn **LOW**, **SEATED & LIT**. Reason chip: `liver-selective · barely diffuses into muscle · most potent · few interactions · 19h (dose anytime).`
- **SIMVASTATIN 80** — an **oil-drop** glyph, `LIPOPHILIC`, the muscle-ache bar drawn **HIGH**, **dimmed**. Disqualifier: `diffuses into muscle → aches that blunt your sessions.`
- A **myth-buster flag** pinned across the pair: `WATER-SOLUBLE = GENTLER ON MUSCLE. If you heard "rosuva is fat-soluble," that's backwards.`
- An **EZETIMIBE "SECOND DOOR"** companion drawn *beside* the statin socket (a second smaller gate): `closes the OTHER door — gut cholesterol absorption. Two mechanisms → keep the statin dose LOW → fewer side effects.`

This single tray teaches the whole grammar — flange (job) vs skirt (fit), seated vs dimmed, reason vs disqualifier, and the "keep the dose low by pairing mechanisms" move — and the rest of the rack repeats the glyph in miniature.

**The over-stack discipline** rides the base of the rack as a quiet **PARTS-COUNT bar / gauge**: `DEFEND THE SYSTEM AGAINST THE COMBINED LOAD — not one ancillary per drug.` The cross-ties above it are the visible reason the count stays low; the gauge ticks amber if the bench is over-seated. (This carries the article's closing "selection principles & over-stacking trap," even though that section is currently truncated on disk.)

This is a deliberately *different instrument* from every prior PE poster but speaks the same locked "Engineering Diagnostic Instrument" language — and it is the natural **catalog** that every 4.1 ladder reaches into at rung 4.

